#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# to_radicals

#% text
# `to_radicals(e)` replaces every `root_sum` in `e` whose polynomial is an irreducible cubic or quartic by the sum over its roots written with radicals.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The roots are the ones `solve` gives by the cubic and quartic formulas, so they may contain complex radicals even when the sum is real.

#% item
# Sums over polynomials of other degrees stay as they are.

#% item
# A root object `root(p, k)` of degree 2 to 32 is written as the radical, among the roots `roots` gives for p, that is nearest to its value at 30 digits, when all of them are radicals (degree 2 to 4, binomials x^n - c, polynomials in x^m of such degrees); otherwise it is kept. An `algebraic_number(r, [c0, c1, ...])` becomes c0 + c1 r + ... with r in radicals.

#% item
# The form of the radicals of a root of a cubic or quartic: each rational factor is distributed over the sum it multiplies (the whole value, and each term of it), and a power `(z^-1)^r` of a non-real number `z` is written `z^-r`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A sum of logarithms over the roots of a pure cubic:

#% cell
to_radicals(root_sum(x => x^3 - 2, x => x*log(z - x)))
#% output
#= log(z - 2^(1/3))*2^(1/3) + log(z - (-1)^(2/3)*2^(1/3))*(-1)^(2/3)*2^(1/3) - log(z + (-1)^(1/3)*2^(1/3))*(-1)^(1/3)*2^(1/3)

#% text
# A root of a binomial of degree 5:

#% cell
to_radicals(root(x => x^5 - 2, 3))
#% output
#= (-1)^(4/5)*2^(1/5)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The numeric value does not change:

#% cell
n(to_radicals(root_sum(x => x^3 + x + 1, x => log(2 - x))))
#% output
#= 2.3978952727983707

#% cell
n(root_sum(x => x^3 + x + 1, x => log(2 - x)))
#% output
#= 2.3978952727983707

#% text
# The rational factors distributed:

#% cell
to_radicals(root(&(&1^3 + &1 + 11), 1))
#% output
#= (-1/3)*2^(1/3)*3^(2/3)*((-99 + 9813^(1/2))^-1)^(1/3) + (1/6)*2^(2/3)*3^(1/3)*(-99 + 9813^(1/2))^(1/3)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `root_sum`, `solve`, `root`
